rete specification after the claim?\"\n  - If Category A or B (zero details/type only) → INVALID METHOD → FAIL Level 2\n  - If Category C (one or more concrete details) → VALID METHOD → PASS Level 2, proceed to Level 3\n\n- **Level 3 (Presentation Quality) asks:** \"Are the provided details COMPLETE?\"\n  - Justification gaps (incomplete details, missing steps) are Level 3 issues\n  - Do NOT reject at Level 2 if ANY concrete specification was provided\n\n**Examples Showing the Boundary:**\n\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 works\" (nothing follows - Category A)\n❌ Level 2 FAIL: \"k=3 using vertical lines\" (type named, but NO concrete details - Category B)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 works: L₁: y=x, L₂: y=2x\" (has equations - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"k=3 via lines at points (1,1), (2,2), (3,3)\" (has points - Category C)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Sunny line through (n,1)\" (one point provided - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n✅ Level 2 PASS: \"Three sunny lines cover 6 rightmost points\" (count+target - Category C, may get JUSTIFICATION_GAP at Level 3)\n\n**WARNING - Common Misclassification:**\n- If solution says \"k=X works\" AND THEN provides equations/points/count → This is Category C (PASS Level 2)\n- Only reject at Level 2 if \"k=X works\" appears WITH NO concrete details following it\n- ONE point is better than ZERO points → proceed to Level 3 for completeness check\n\n**STEP 4: Handle Context-Dependent Claims (CRITICAL CALIBRATION)**\n\nSome mathematical claims are true in one context but false in another. Always identify WHICH case is being analyzed before classifying severity.\n\n**Example Pattern:**\n```\nClaim: \"The three rightmost columns force the use of a vertical line for column n-2\"\nContext A (k≤3): This claim is TRUE\nContext B (k≥4): This claim is FALSE\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≤3 case:\n  → Claim is TRUE in this context\n  → If scope not explicit: JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5, ACCEPTABLE)\n  → NOT a CRITICAL_ERROR just because it's false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nConsider a $2025\\times2025$ grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of difference sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile.\n\nDetermine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n***\\n\\nLet $n=2025$.  The grid consists of the set $[n]\\\\times[n]$ of unit squares, where $[n]=\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$.\\n\\n### 1.  Uncovered squares form a permutation matrix\\nBecause each row must contain exactly one uncovered square and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, there exists a bijection $\\\\pi\\\\colon [n]\\\\to[n]$ such that the uncovered squares are\\n\\\\[\\nU=\\\\{\\\\,(i,\\\\pi(i))\\\\mid i\\\\in[n] \\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nAll other squares are to be covered by tiles.\\n\\n### 2.  Tiles as bicliques\\nConsider the bipartite graph $G$ with vertex classes $R=[n]$ (rows) and $C=[n]$ (columns).  An edge $(i,j)$ is present iff the square $(i,j)$ is to be covered, i.e. $j\\\\neq\\\\pi(i)$.  Hence\\n\\\\[\\nG = K_{n,n}\\\\;\\\\setminus\\\\;M,\\\\qquad M=\\\\{\\\\,(i,\\\\pi(i))\\\\mid i\\\\in[n] \\\\}\\n\\\\]\\nis the complete bipartite graph with the perfect matching $M$ removed.\\n\\nA rectangular tile covering a set $R'\\\\subseteq R$ of rows and a set $C'\\\\subseteq C$ of columns corresponds exactly to the complete bipartite subgraph (biclique) $R'\\\\times C'$ of $G$.  Because the tile must avoid all uncovered squares, the biclique must avoid the edges of $M$.  Consequently\\n\\\\[\\nC'\\\\subseteq C\\\\setminus\\\\{\\\\pi(i)\\\\mid i\\\\in R'\\\\},\\\\qquad\\\\text{so }|C'|\\\\le n-|R'|.\\n\\\\]\\nThus every tile is a biclique $R'\\\\times C'$ with $|R'|+|C'|\\\\le n$.\\n\\nThe problem is therefore: *cover all edges of $G$ with the smallest number of such bicliques.*\\n\\n### 3.  A family of critical cells\\nFor each row $i$ with $\\\\pi(i)\\\\neq n$ define the cell\\n\\\\[\\na_i = (i,\\\\,\\\\pi(i)+1).\\n\\\\]\\nFor each column $j$ with $\\\\pi^{-1}(j)\\\\neq n$ define the cell\\n\\\\[\\nb_j = (\\\\,\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1,\\\\,j\\\\).\\n\\\\]\\nThe two families are disjoint; there are exactly $(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2$ such cells.  Geometrically $a_i$ is the unit square immediately to the right of the uncovered square in row $i$, and $b_j$ is the unit square immediately below the uncovered square in column $j$.\\n\\n### 4.  Lower bound: a biclique can contain at most one critical cell\\nAssume a biclique $B=R'\\\\times C'$ contains two distinct critical cells.  Three cases are possible.\\n\\n**Case 1.** $B$ contains $a_i$ and $a_k$ with $i\\\\neq k$.  Then $i,k\\\\in R'$ and $\\\\pi(i)+1,\\\\,\\\\pi(k)+1\\\\in C'$.  Without loss of generality $i<k$.  The column interval $C'$ contains both $\\\\pi(i)+1$ and $\\\\pi(k)+1$, hence it contains every integer between them.  If $\\\\pi(i)<\\\\pi(k)$, then $\\\\pi(k)$ lies between $\\\\pi(i)+1$ and $\\\\pi(k)+1$, so $(k,\\\\pi(k))\\\\in B$, contradicting $B\\\\subseteq G$.  If $\\\\pi(i)>\\\\pi(k)$, then $\\\\pi(i)$ lies between $\\\\pi(k)+1$ and $\\\\pi(i)+1$, and $(i,\\\\pi(i))\\\\in B$, again a contradiction.\\n\\n**Case 2.** $B$ contains $b_j$ and $b_{j'}$ with $j\\\\neq j'$.  This is symmetric to Case 1 after swapping the roles of rows and columns.\\n\\n**Case 3.** $B$ contains $a_i$ and $b_j$.  Then $i\\\\in R'$, $j\\\\in C'$, $\\\\pi(i)+1\\\\in C'$ and $\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1\\\\in R'$.  If $j\\\\le \\\\pi(i)$, then the column $j$ lies in $C'$ and the row $\\\\pi^{-1}(j)$ lies in $R'$, so the uncovered square $(\\\\pi^{-1}(j),j)\\\\in B$, a contradiction.  If $j>\\\\pi(i)$, then the column $\\\\pi(i)$ lies in $C'$ and the row $i$ lies in $R'$, so the uncovered square $(i,\\\\pi(i))\\\\in B$, again a contradiction.\\n\\nIn every case a biclique containing two critical cells would also contain an uncovered square, which is impossible.  Hence **any biclique can contain at most one critical cell**.\\n\\nSince the $2n-2$ critical cells must all be covered, at least $2n-2$ bicliques (tiles) are necessary.\\n\\n### 5.  Upper bound: a covering with $2n-2$ tiles\\nWe now exhibit a family of $2n-2$ bicliques that covers all edges of $G$.\\n\\n- For each row $i$ with $\\\\pi(i)\\\\neq n$ define the horizontal biclique\\n  \\\\[H_i = \\\\{i\\\\}\\\\times\\\\{\\\\pi(i)+1,\\\\pi(i)+2,\\\\dots,n\\\\}.\\n  \\\\]  Its leftmost cell is precisely $a_i$.\\n\\n- For each column $j$ with $\\\\pi^{-1}(j)\\\\neq n$ define the vertical biclique\\n  \\\\[V_j = \\\\{\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1,\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+2,\\\\dots,n\\\\}\\\\times\\\\{j\\\\}.\\n  \\\\]  Its topmost cell is precisely $b_j$.\\n\\nThe families $\\\\{H_i\\\\}$ and $\\\\{V_j\\\\}$ are disjoint because a cell $(r,c)$ belongs to a horizontal biclique exactly when $c>\\\\pi(r)$, while it belongs to a vertical biclique exactly when $r>\\\\pi^{-1}(c)$.  These two conditions are mutually exclusive, so each cell of $G$ lies in exactly one of the bicliques.\\n\\nConsequently the $n-1$ horizontal bicliques together with the $n-1$ vertical bicliques cover **all** edges of $G$, i.e. all squares that are not uncovered.  The total number of tiles is\\n\\\\[(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n### 6.  Minimality\\nThe lower‑bound argument of Section 4 shows that no tiling can use fewer than $2n-2$ tiles, while the explicit construction of Section 5 achieves exactly $2n-2$ tiles.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n\\\\[\\\\boxed{2n-2}.\\\\]\\n\\nFor the given grid $n=2025$, so the answer is\\n\\\\[2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\\\\]\\n\\nThus Matilda must place at least and at most $4048$ rectangular tiles, and this number is optimal.\\n\"}\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Explicit verification for p=3 provided ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Gap - The solution should explicitly state \"since p>3 is prime, it's not divisible by 3, so p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\" for complete rigor. The claim is mathematically 