overed squares might be uncovered by both. But note that for any square (r,c) that is left of uncovered square in its row (c < pi(r)), we have c is some column. The uncovered square in column c is at row pi^{-1}(c). Since c < pi(r), we have pi(r) > c. But we don't know relation between r and pi^{-1}(c). Could be r > pi^{-1}(c) or r < pi^{-1}(c). If r > pi^{-1}(c), then (r,c) is covered by V_c (since it's below the uncovered square in column c). If r < pi^{-1}(c), then it's above the uncovered square in column c, and also left of uncovered square in its row. So such squares would not be covered by either H_i or V_j. Are there such squares? Let's analyze.\n\nConsider the permutation matrix pi. For any (r,c) not equal to (i, pi(i)), we have either c > pi(r) or c < pi(r). If c > pi(r), covered by H_r. If c < pi(r), then consider s = pi^{-1}(c). Since pi(s) = c. Compare r and s. If r > s, then (r,c) is covered by V_c. If r < s, then (r,c) is above the uncovered square in column c, and left of uncovered square in row r. So not covered by either.\n\nThus the construction fails to cover squares where r < s and c < pi(r). Let's see if such squares exist. Since pi is a permutation, we can think of the permutation matrix. The condition r < s and c < pi(r) means that the point (r,c) lies in the region above the uncovered square in column c (i.e., above row s) and to the left of the uncovered square in row r (i.e., left of column pi(r)). This region is the \"northwest\" region relative to the uncovered squares. For a given permutation, there may be squares in that region. Indeed, consider any permutation that is not monotone decreasing. For example, pi = identity: pi(i) = i. Then uncovered squares are diagonal. For a cell (r,c) with r < c (i.e., above diagonal? Actually r < c means row less than column, so cell is above diagonal? Let's think: row index increasing downwards, column index increasing rightwards. So the diagonal goes from top-left to bottom-right. The region above d