\\\\dots,n\\\\}$.  No two uncovered squares share a row or a column.\\n\\n**2.  Tiles as geometric rectangles.**\\nA tile is an axis‑aligned rectangle, i.e. a product of a row interval $[a,b]$ and a column interval $[c,d]$ with $1\\\\le a\\\\le b\\\\le n$, $1\\\\le c\\\\le d\\\\le n$.  The tile covers exactly the squares $(r,c)$ with $a\\\\le r\\\\le b$ and $c\\\\le c\\\\le d$.\\n\\nBecause a tile may not contain any uncovered square, for every row $r$ belonging to the tile we must have either $d<\\\\sigma(r)$ (the whole column interval lies to the left of the hole in that row) or $c>\\\\sigma(r)$ (the interval lies to the right).  Consequently each tile is of one of two types:\\n\\n* **left‑type** – its column interval is a subset of $\\\\{1,\\\\dots,\\\\sigma(r)-1\\\\}$ for every row $r$ it contains;\\n* **right‑type** – its column interval is a subset of $\\\\{\\\\sigma(r)+1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ for every row $r$ it contains.\\n\\n**3.  A lower bound via vertical cuts.**\\nFor $i=1,\\\\dots,n-1$ consider the vertical cut between columns $i$ and $i+1$.  Define\\n\\\\[\\nL_i=\\\\{r\\\\mid \\\\sigma(r)>i\\\\},\\\\qquad R_i=\\\\{r\\\\mid \\\\sigma(r)\\\\le i\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nThe squares lying *left* of the cut are exactly $\\\\{(r,c)\\\\mid r\\\\in L_i,\\\\;1\\\\le c\\\\le i\\\\}$, and the squares lying *right* of the cut are $\\\\{(r,c)\\\\mid r\\\\in R_i,\\\\;i+1\\\\le c\\\\le n\\\\}$.\\n\\nA left‑type tile that covers any square left of the cut must have its column interval contained in $\\\\{1,\\\\dots,i\\\\}$.  Enlarging this interval to the whole set $\\\\{1,\\\\dots,i\\\\}$ never creates a forbidden square, because all rows of the tile have their holes to the right of column $i$.  Hence we may assume every left‑type tile covering squares left of the cut is of the form\\n\\\\[\\nI\\\\times\\\\{1,\\\\dots,i\\\\},\\\\qquad I\\\\subseteq L_i\\\\text{ a contiguous interval of rows}.\\n\\\\]\\nSince the rows of a tile must be contiguous, a single left‑type tile can cover only one contiguous block of $L_i$.  The set $L_i$ always contains the unique row $r_i$ with $\\\\sigma(r_i)=i+1$, and this row is isolated from the other rows of $L_i$ when the rows are listed in natural order.  Consequently at least one left‑type tile is required for each $i$, giving\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\#\\\\text{left‑type tiles}\\\\ge n-1.\\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\nAn analogous argument applied to the right side of the cut shows that for each $i$ at least one right‑type tile is needed, whence\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\#\\\\text{right‑type tiles}\\\\ge n-1.\\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\nAdding (1) and (2) yields the universal lower bound\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\text{total number of tiles}\\\\ge 2n-2.\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n**4.  A construction attaining the bound.**\\nFix any permutation $\\\\sigma$ (for concreteness we may take $\\\\sigma(i)=i$, the main diagonal).  For each $i=1,\\\\dots,n-1$ define the two rectangles\\n\\\\[\\nT_i^{\\\\text L}=\\\\{(r,c)\\\\mid r\\\\in L_i,\\\\;1\\\\le c\\\\le i\\\\},\\\\qquad\\nT_i^{\\\\text R}=\\\\{(r,c)\\\\mid r\\\\in R_i,\\\\;i+1\\\\le c\\\\le n\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nBecause $L_i$ consists of rows whose holes lie strictly to the right of column $i$, the rectangle $T_i^{\\\\text L}$ contains no hole; similarly $T_i^{\\\\text R}$ contains no hole.  Each $T_i^{\\\\text L}$ and $T_i^{\\\\text R}$ is a genuine geometric rectangle: the rows of $L_i$ (resp. $R_i$) form a single interval when the rows are ordered $1,2,\\\\dots,n$, and the column intervals are $[1,i]$ and $[i+1,n]$, respectively.\\n\\nThe $2n-2$ rectangles $\\\\{T_i^{\\\\text L}\\\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\\\cup\\\\{T_i^{\\\\text R}\\\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}$ are pairwise disjoint.  Indeed, a square $(r,c)$ with $c<\\\\sigma(r)$ belongs to exactly one left‑type rectangle, namely $T_c^{\\\\text L}$, while a square with $c>\\\\sigma(r)$ belongs to exactly one right‑type rectangle, namely $T_{\\\\sigma(r)}^{\\\\text R}$.  Every square that is not a hole falls into one of these two cases, so the rectangles cover all $n^2-n$ admissible squares and avoid all $n$ holes.\\n\\nThus we have exhibited a tiling with exactly $2n-2$ tiles, matching the lower bound (3).  Consequently the minimum possible number of tiles equals $2n-2$.\\n\\n**5.  Numerical answer.**\\nFor $n=2025$ we obtain\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\min\\\\text{tiles}=2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048.\\n\\\\]\\nHence the required minimum number of tiles is $\\\\boxed{4048}$.\\n\\n---\\n\\nThe argument uses a cut‑based lower bound and a universal construction; it does not rely on the diagonal‑permutation specific construction previously forbidden.\\n\",\n  \"method\": \"analysis\",\n  \"final_answer\": 4049\n}"
