y one contiguous block of $L_i$.  The set $L_i$ always contains the unique row $r_i$ with $\\\\sigma(r_i)=i+1$, and this row is isolated from the other rows of $L_i$ when the rows are listed in natural order.  Consequently at least one left‑type tile is required for each $i$, giving\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\#\\\\text{left‑type tiles}\\\\ge n-1.\\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\nAn analogous argument applied to the right side of the cut shows that for each $i$ at least one right‑type tile is needed, whence\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\#\\\\text{right‑type tiles}\\\\ge n-1.\\\\tag{2}\\n\\\\]\\nAdding (1) and (2) yields the universal lower bound\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\text{total number of tiles}\\\\ge 2n-2.\\\\tag{3}\\n\\\\]\\n\\n**4.  A construction attaining the bound.**\\nFix any permutation $\\\\sigma$ (for concreteness we may take $\\\\sigma(i)=i$, the main diagonal).  For each $i=1,\\\\dots,n-1$ define the two rectangles\\n\\\\[\\nT_i^{\\\\text L}=\\\\{(r,c)\\\\mid r\\\\in L_i,\\\\;1\\\\le c\\\\le i\\\\},\\\\qquad\\nT_i^{\\\\text R}=\\\\{(r,c)\\\\mid r\\\\in R_i,\\\\;i+1\\\\le c\\\\le n\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nBecause $L_i$ consists of rows whose holes lie strictly to the right of column $i$, the rectangle $T_i^{\\\\text L}$ contains no hole; similarly $T_i^{\\\\text R}$ contains no hole.  Each $T_i^{\\\\text L}$ and $T_i^{\\\\text R}$ is a genuine geometric rectangle: the rows of $L_i$ (resp. $R_i$) form a single interval when the rows are ordered $1,2,\\\\dots,n$, and the column intervals are $[1,i]$ and $[i+1,n]$, respectively.\\n\\nThe $2n-2$ rectangles $\\\\{T_i^{\\\\text L}\\\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\\\cup\\\\{T_i^{\\\\text R}\\\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}$ are pairwise disjoint.  Indeed, a square $(r,c)$ with $c<\\\\sigma(r)$ belongs to exactly one left‑type rectangle, namely $T_c^{\\\\text L}$, while a square with $c>\\\\sigma(r)$ belongs to exactly one right‑type rectangle, namely $T_{\\\\sigma(r)}^{\\\\text R}$.  Every square that is not a hole falls into one of these two cases, so the rectangles cover all $n^2-n$ admissible squar