fine $s$ to be the largest index $\\\\ge r$ such that for every row $t$ with $r\\\\le t\\\\le s$ we have $\\\\sigma(t)>c$.  (Equivalently, the holes of all rows $r,\\\\dots,s$ lie strictly to the right of column $c$.)\\nCreate the rectangle\\n$$T=[r,\\\\,s]\\\\times[1,\\\\,c].$$\\nBecause $c<\\\\sigma(t)$ for all $t\\\\in[r,s]$, $T$ is left\u2011type and contains no hole.  Mark rows $r,\\\\dots,s$ as covered on their left side and continue the scan with row $s+1$.\\n\\nEach time we start a new rectangle the value of $c$ strictly decreases: the next rectangle starts at a row $r'$ where $\\\\sigma(r')\\\\le c$, so its rightmost column $c' = \\\\sigma(r')-1 \\\\le c-1$.  Since $c$ is an integer between $0$ and $n-1$, this process can produce at most $n-1$ rectangles.  By construction the rectangles are pairwise disjoint and their union is exactly $L$.\\n\\n*Partition of $R$.*\\nPerform the symmetric procedure on the right side.  Scan rows from top to bottom.  When encountering a row $r$ whose right part is uncovered, let\\n$$d=\\\\sigma(r)+1,$$\\nthe leftmost column belonging to $R$ in that row.  Let $s$ be the largest index $\\\\ge r$ such that $\\\\sigma(t)<d$ for all $t\\\\in[r,s]$ (i.e., the holes of those rows lie strictly to the left of column $d$).  Create the rectangle\\n$$U=[r,\\\\,s]\\\\times[d,\\\\,n].$$\\nAgain $U$ is right\u2011type, contains no hole, and the process yields at most $n-1$ such rectangles, covering all of $R$.\\n\\n*Combination.*\\nThe families $\\\\{T\\\\}$ and $\\\\{U\\\\}$ are disjoint because every $T$ lies entirely in columns $\\\\le i$ for some $i$, while every $U$ lies entirely in columns $\\\\ge i+1$ for the same $i$, and the two families never overlap a hole.  Their union covers $L\\\\cup R$, i.e., all squares that must be tiled.  Hence we have produced a tiling with at most $(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2$ tiles.\\n\\n**4.  Conclusion.**\\nThe lower bound (3) shows that no tiling can use fewer than $2n-2$ tiles, and the construction above achieves exactly $2n-2$ tiles for any permutation $\\\\sigma$.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n$$2n-2=2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048.$$\\n\\nThus Matilda can always achieve the required condition with $4048$ tiles, and she cannot do it with fewer.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\\nReview the Summary and Detailed Solution to ensure:\\n- The lower\u2011bound argument correctly uses distinct cuts and the fact that each left\u2011type (right\u2011type) rectangle can be assigned to at most one cut, guaranteeing at least $n-1$ rectangles of each type.\\n- The greedy construction for $L$ and $R$ indeed produces at most $n-1$ rectangles of each type, that the rectangles are disjoint, and that they avoid all holes.\\n- No reliance on a specific diagonal placement of holes; the construction works for any permutation $\\\\sigma$.\\n- All statements are justified and no steps are omitted.\\n\",\n  \"method\": \"analysis\",\n  \"final_answer\": 4049\n}"
