mutation.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**1. Lower bound $\\\\;t\\\\ge 2n-2$**\\n\\nConsider a fixed row $i$.  The uncovered cell $(i,\\\\sigma(i))$ splits the row into at most two contiguous blocks of covered squares:\\n- If $\\\\sigma(i)=1$ (the uncovered cell is the leftmost one) the whole row except that cell is a single contiguous block, so the row can be covered by **one** horizontal tile.\\n- If $\\\\sigma(i)=n$ (the uncovered cell is the rightmost one) the same conclusion holds.\\n- If $2\\\\le \\\\sigma(i)\\\\le n-1$, the row is divided into a left block of length $\\\\sigma(i)-1$ and a right block of length $n-\\\\sigma(i)$.  Both blocks are non‑empty, therefore the row needs **at least two** horizontal tiles.\\n\\nSince $\\\\sigma$ is a permutation, the column $1$ can be the image of at most one row, and the column $n$ can be the image of at most one row.  Consequently at most two rows have their uncovered square at an endpoint of the row; all remaining $n-2$ rows have the uncovered square in an interior column and therefore need at least two tiles each.\\n\\nHence any admissible tiling uses at least\\n\\\\[\\n2\\\\cdot (n-2)\\\\;+\\\\;1\\\\;+\\\\;1\\\\;=\\\\;2n-2\\n\\\\]\\ntiles.  For $n=2025$ this gives the lower bound $t\\\\ge 4048$.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**2. Upper bound $\\\\;t\\\\le 2n-2$**\\n\\nWe now exhibit a tiling that uses exactly $2n-2$ tiles.  Choose the permutation\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sigma(i)=n+1-i \\\\qquad (1\\\\le i\\\\le n),\\n\\\\]\\ni.e. place the uncovered squares on the anti‑diagonal: the uncovered cell in row $i$ is $(i,n+1-i)$.  In particular, the uncovered square of row $1$ is at column $n$, and that of row $n$ is at column $1$.\\n\\n*Row $1$* and *row $n$*: each of these rows has its uncovered square at an endpoint, so the remaining $n-1$ cells form a single contiguous segment.  Tile each of these rows by one horizontal $1\\\\times (n-