wer bound argument says impossible. Is the lower bound proof correct? Let's examine the proof: It defines right-critical cells a_i = (i, pi(i)+1) for rows where pi(i) != n. Similarly below-critical cells b_j = (pi^{-1}(j)+1, j) for columns where pi^{-1}(j) != n. It claims all critical cells are distinct, total 2n-2. Then shows any tile can contain at most one critical cell, by analyzing three cases. The argument seems plausible. Need to verify if there is any oversight: Could a tile contain two critical cells of different types but not contain any uncovered square? The proof addresses case 3: one right-critical and one below-critical. It argues that if tile contains both, then it must also contain an uncovered square. The reasoning: rectangle contains rows i and pi^{-1}(j)+1, columns pi(i)+1 and j. If j <= pi(i), then column j is inside column interval, and with row i gives uncovered square (i, pi(i)). If j > pi(i), then column pi(i) is inside column interval, and with row pi^{-1}(j) gives uncovered square (pi^{-1}(j), j). This seems correct. However, need to verify that the rectangle indeed contains those rows and columns. The rectangle contains rows from min(i, pi^{-1}(j)+1) to max(i, pi^{-1}(j)+1). Similarly columns from min(pi(i)+1, j) to max(pi(i)+1, j). So indeed if j <= pi(i), then column j is between pi(i)+1 and j? Wait, if j <= pi(i), then j <= pi(i). The column interval is [j, pi(i)+1] (since min = j, max = pi(i)+1). So column j is included. Row i is included. So (i, j) is inside rectangle. But they claim uncovered square (i, pi(i)) is inside rectangle. Let's check: column pi(i) is between j and pi(i)+1? Since j <= pi(i) < pi(i)+1, pi(i) is also between j and pi(i)+1, so column pi(i) is inside column interval. Row i is inside row interval. So (i, pi(i)) is inside rectangle. Good.\n\nIf j > pi(i), then column pi(i) is between pi(i)+1? Actually pi(i)+1 > pi(i). The column interval is [pi(i)+1, j] (since min = pi(i)+1, max = j). Column pi(i) is less than pi(i)+1, so not inside column interval. But they claim column pi(i) is inside column interval. Wait, need to examine case j > pi(i). Then column interval is [pi(i)+1, j] (since pi(i)+1 <= j). Column pi(i) is not inside that interval because it's less than the lower bound. However they claim column pi(i) lies in column interval. That seems a mistake. Let's re-evaluate: The rectangle contains columns from min(pi(i)+1, j) to max(pi(i)+1, j). If j > pi(i)+1, then min = pi(i)+1, max = j. So column pi(i) is not in [pi(i)+1, j]. So the argument that (i, pi(i)) is inside rectangle fails. But they consider column pi(i) (the uncovered square's column) maybe inside because rectangle also contains row pi^{-1}(j)+1? Let's examine the rectangle's rows: includes rows i and pi^{-1}(j)+1. The uncovered square (pi^{-1}(j), j) has row pi^{-1}(j) (which is one less than pi^{-1}(j)+1). Row pi^{-1}(j) is not necessarily inside the rectangle. The rectangle includes rows from min(i, pi^{-1}(j)+1) to max(i, pi^{-1}(j)+1). If pi^{-1}(j) < pi^{-1}(j)+1, obviously pi^{-1}(j) is not inside unless pi^{-1}(j) = i or pi^{-1}(j)+1 = i. So the argument may be flawed.\n\nLet's examine the geometry: Suppose we have a right-critical cell a_i = (i, pi(i)+1). So the uncovered square is at (i, pi(i)). The right-critical cell is directly to the right (same row, next column). Similarly, below-critical cell b_j = (pi^{-1}(j)+1, j) is directly below the uncovered square at (pi^{-1}(j), j). So the rectangle containing both a_i and b_j must include the row i and row pi^{-1}(j)+1, and column pi(i)+1 and column j. So the rectangle's row interval includes rows between i and pi^{-1}(j)+1 inclusive. The column interval includes columns between pi(i)+1 and j inclusive. The rectangle may be oriented such that i <= pi^{-1}(j)+1 or vice versa; similarly for columns.\n\nNow, we need to see if the rectangle must contain an uncovered square. Consider the case where i < pi^{-1}(j)+1 and pi(i)+1 < j. Then rectangle includes rows i,...,pi^{-1}(j)+1 and columns pi(i)+1,...,j. Does it contain any uncovered square? The uncovered squares are at (i, pi(i)) and (pi^{-1}(j), j). The first has column pi(i), which is one less than pi(i)+1, so not in column interval. The second has row pi^{-1}(j), which is one less than pi^{-1}(j)+1, so not in row interval. So neither uncovered square is inside rectangle. However, there could be other uncovered squares (different rows/columns) inside rectangle. But the rectangle's rows and columns are limited to those intervals; any other uncovered square (k, pi(k)) would have row k in the row interval and column pi(k) in the column interval. Could there be such a k? Possibly. But the proof claims that there must be at least one uncovered square inside rectangle. Is that always true? Let's test with a concrete example to see if a rectangle can contain both a right-critical and a below-critical cell without containing any uncovered square.\n\nTake n=4, permutation pi = (1->2, 2->4, 3->1, 4->3). Let's compute critical cells: Right-critical cells: for rows i where pi(i) != n (i.e., pi(i) != 4). pi(1)=2 => a_1 = (1,3). pi(2)=4 => no right-critical for row 2. pi(3)=1 => a_3 = (3,2). pi(4)=3 => a_4 = (4,4)?? Wait pi(4)=3, not n, so a_4 = (4,4). Actually pi(4)=3, so a_4 = (4,4). Below-critical cells: for columns j where pi^{-1}(j) != n (i.e., column not containing uncovered square in row n). Compute pi^{-1}: pi^{-1}(1)=3, pi^{-1}(2)=1, pi^{-1}(3)=4, pi^{-1}(4)=2. For each column j != pi(n)?? Actually we need columns j where pi^{-1}(j) != n (i.e., column not containing uncovered square in row n). pi^{-1}(j) != n means pi^{-1}(j) != 4. So for j=1, pi^{-1}(1)=3 !=4 => b_1 = (4,1). For j=2, pi^{-1}(2)=1 !=4 => b_2 = (2,2). For j=3, pi^{-1}(3)=4 => no below-critical for column 3. For j=4, pi^{-1}(4)=2 !=4 => b_4 = (3,4). So we have critical cells: a_1=(1,3), a_3=(3,2), a_4=(4,4); b_1=(4,1), b_2=(2,2), b_4=(3,4). That's 6 critical cells (2n-2=6). Now can we find a rectangle containing a right-critical and a below-critical cell but no uncovered squares? Let's try a rectangle containing a_1=(1,3) and b_2=(2,2). The rectangle would have rows 1..2 and columns 2..3. This rectangle includes squares (1,2), (1,3), (2,2), (2,3). Uncovered squares: (1,2) is uncovered? Actually pi(1)=2, so (1,2) is uncovered. Indeed (1,2) is uncovered, which is inside rectangle. So that rectangle contains uncovered square. Good.\n\nTry a_3=(3,2) and b_1=(4,1). Rows 3..4, columns 1..2. Contains squares (3,1),(3,2),(4,1),(4,2). Uncovered squares: (3,1) is uncovered? pi(3)=1, so (3,1) uncovered. So rectangle contains uncovered.\n\nTry a_4=(4,4) and b_4=(3,4). Rows 3..4, columns 4..4 (single column). Contains squares (3,4),(4,4). Uncovered squares: (4,3) is uncovered? Actually pi(4)=3, so (4,3) uncovered, not in rectangle. (3,1) uncovered, not in rectangle. (2,4) uncovered? pi(2)=4, so (2,4) uncovered, not in rectangle. So rectangle (3,4)-(4,4) contains (3,4) which is below-critical, (4,4) right-critical, but does it contain any uncovered? (3,4) is not uncovered (since uncovered at (3,1)). (4,4) is not uncovered (since uncovered at (4,3)). So rectangle contains no uncovered squares! This seems to be a counterexample to the claim that any rectangle containing a right-critical and a below-critical cell must contain an uncovered square.\n\nBut note that a_4 = (4,4) is a right-critical cell because pi(4)=3, so pi(4)+1=4. b_4 = (3,4) is a below-critical cell because pi^{-1}(4)=2, so pi^{-1}(4)+1=3, column 4. Indeed both are critical cells. The rectangle covering rows 3-4 and column 4 includes exactly those two cells. Does it contain any uncovered square? Uncovered squares are (1,2), (2,4), (3,1), (4,3). None of those are in rows 3-4 and column 4? (2,4) is row 2, not in rectangle. (4,3) is column 3, not in rectangle. So indeed rectangle contains no uncovered squares. This contradicts the claim in the solution's lower bound proof.\n\nBut wait, does the rectangle contain both a right-critical and a below-critical cell? Yes. So the lower bound proof is flawed: there exists a rectangle that contains two critical cells (one right-critical, one below-critical) without containing any uncovered square. So the claim \"any tile can contain at most one critical cell\" is false. Therefore the lower bound of 2n-2 may not hold. The solution's lower bound argument is invalid.\n\nThus Level 2 (Reasoning Validity) will be affected: The solution uses a valid principle? The method is constructing a lower bound using a combinatorial argument. However, the argument contains a flaw: the claim that any tile can contain at most one critical cell is false. This is a logical error in the reasoning, not just a presentation gap. This is a critical error in reasoning (invalid method? Actually the method is valid, but the specific claim is false). According to Level 2, we must check if the solution uses valid mathematical principles. The principle used is \"a tile can contain at most one critical cell\". This is a claim that is proven incorrectly. The proof is invalid; the reasoning is flawed. Does this count as using an invalid principle? The principle is not a standard principle; it's a derived claim. The proof is invalid, so the reasoning is invalid. According to Level 2, if reasoning uses invalid principles or contains logical errors, we should mark FAIL. The solution's lower bound is based on a false claim, so the reasoning is invalid.\n\nThus Level 2: FAIL.\n\nBut we must check Level 1.5 for optimality: Since the lower bound is suspect, we might suspect the answer is not optimal. However, we need to follow the hierarchical decision tree: Level 1 passed, then Level 1.5. In Level 1.5, we must test small cases. For n=3, the solution's construction yields 4 tiles. Could there be a tiling with 3 tiles? Let's try to find a tiling for n=3 with 3 tiles. The lower bound says at least 4, but we suspect lower bound is false, maybe 3 is possible. Let's attempt to find a tiling with 3 tiles for n=3. Let's think: We need uncovered squares form a permutation matrix of size 3. There are 6 squares to cover. Tiles can be rectangles covering multiple squares. With 3 tiles, each tile must cover on average 2 squares. Could we cover all 6 squares with 3 rectangles without covering any uncovered squares? Let's try to find a configuration.\n\nWe need to pick a permutation pi of {1,2,3}. Let's try pi = identity: uncovered squares at (1,1), (2,2), (3,3). Then the remaining squares are all off-diagonal: (1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,3),(3,1),(3,2). Can we tile these with 3 rectangles? For instance, tile 1: rectangle covering (1,2),(1,3) (row 1, columns 2-3) is a 1x2 rectangle. Tile 2: rectangle covering (2,1),(2,3) is not contiguous in columns (1 and 3) so can't be a rectangle. Could use rectangle covering (2,1),(3,1) (col 1, rows 2-3) is a 2x1 rectangle. Tile 3: rectangle covering (2,3),(3,2) cannot be a rectangle. So identity may not work.\n\nTry permutation pi = (1->2,2->3,3->1). Uncovered squares: (1,2),(2,3),(3,1). The remaining squares: (1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,2),(3,3). Can we tile with 3 rectangles? Let's see: rectangle covering (1,1),(2,1),(3,1)? No, (3,1) is uncovered. So cannot include column 1 rows 1-3. Maybe rectangle covering (1,1),(1,3) not contiguous. Let's try rectangle covering (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) is diagonal, not rectangle. Hard.\n\nTry pi = (1->3,2->1,3->2). Uncovered squares: (1,3),(2,1),(3,2). Remaining squares: (1,1),(1,2),(2,2),(2,3),(3,1),(3,3). Could we tile with 3 rectangles? Perhaps rectangle covering (1,1),(1,2) (row1 col1-2). Rectangle covering (2,2),(2,3) (row2 col2-3). Rectangle covering (3,1),(3,3) not contiguous. Could use rectangle covering (3,1),(3,3) not possible. Could use rectangle covering (3,1),(2,1) but (2,1) is uncovered. So maybe not.\n\nBut maybe there is a tiling with 3 tiles for n=3. Let's search systematically: The uncovered squares are a permutation matrix; the other squares form a bipartite complement. We need to partition the 6 squares into 3 rectangles. Each rectangle must be axis-aligned and cannot include any uncovered squares. Let's try to see if we can cover each row's uncovered square's right side and each column's uncovered square's below side with rectangles that combine. The solution's construction uses 2 horizontal and 2 vertical rectangles, total 4. But maybe we can combine some horizontal and vertical into larger rectangles that contain two critical cells but not uncovered squares, as we found a counterexample for n=4. For n=3, maybe we can combine a right-critical and below-critical cell that are adjacent (forming a 2x1 or 1x2 rectangle) without covering uncovered squares. Let's examine the critical cells for n=3 for a given permutation. For identity pi = (1,2,3). Right-critical cells: a_1=(1,2), a_2=(2,3). Below-critical cells: b_1=(2,1), b_2=(3,2). That's 4 critical cells. We can pair a_1=(1,2) with b_2=(3,2) to form a vertical rectangle covering rows 1-3, column 2. But column 2 includes uncovered square (2,2). Indeed (2,2) is uncovered. So that rectangle would contain uncovered square, not allowed. Pair a_2=(2,3) with b_1=(2,1) would form horizontal rectangle covering row 2, columns 1-3. That includes uncovered square (2,2). Not allowed. Pair a_1 with b_1: a_1=(1,2), b_1=(2,1). Rectangle rows 1-2, columns 1-2 includes uncovered squares (1,1) and (2,2). Not allowed. Pair a_2 with b_2: rows 2-3, columns 2-3 includes uncovered squares (2,2) and (3,3). Not allowed. So for identity permutation, any rectangle containing a right-critical and a below-critical cell will also contain an uncovered square. So the lower bound holds for that case.\n\nBut the counterexample we found for n=4 used a right-critical and a below-critical cell that are aligned in same column, but the uncovered squares in that column are not adjacent. Let's examine the geometry: a_4=(4,4) and b_4=(3,4). They are adjacent vertically (row 3 and 4, column 4). The uncovered squares in column 4 are at (2,4) (since pi(2)=4). Indeed uncovered square (2,4) is in column 4 but row 2, which is not in the rectangle rows 3-4. So the rectangle does not contain that uncovered square. So the rectangle contains two critical cells but no uncovered squares. So the claim fails for n>=4.\n\nThus the lower bound argument is flawed for n>=4. Therefore the answer 2n-2 may not be minimal; perhaps fewer tiles are possible. So the solution's answer is likely not correct.\n\nThus Level 1.5: we need to test small cases to see if alternative approach yields fewer tiles. For n=4, the solution's construction yields 6 tiles. But we can try to find a tiling with 5 tiles (or maybe 4?). Let's attempt to find a tiling for n=4 with fewer than 6 tiles. The counterexample suggests we can combine a right-critical and below-critical cell into a single tile, reducing tile count by 1. Since there are 2n-2 critical cells, each tile can cover at most 2 critical cells if we can pair them appropriately without covering uncovered squares. If we can pair up all critical cells into n-1 tiles (each tile covering 2 critical cells), we would need n-1 tiles. But is that possible? Let's explore.\n\nWe have 2n-2 critical cells: n-1 right-critical and n-1 below-critical. If we can pair each right-critical with a below-critical that share the same column (or row) and are adjacent (i.e., the rectangle formed by them does not contain any uncovered square), we could reduce tile count. The example for n=4 shows that a right-critical cell a_i = (i, pi(i)+1) and below-critical cell b_j = (pi^{-1}(j)+1, j) can be paired if the uncovered square in column j is not between rows i and pi^{-1}(j)+1. That is, if the uncovered square (pi^{-1}(j), j) lies outside the rectangle's row interval. Similarly, for column j = pi(i)+1 (i.e., the column of a_i), the below-critical cell b_{pi(i)+1} = (pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1)+1, pi(i)+1). The rectangle formed by a_i and b_{pi(i)+1} will have rows i and pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1)+1, columns pi(i)+1 and pi(i)+1 (single column). So it's a vertical rectangle covering rows i..pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1)+1 in column pi(i)+1. The uncovered square in that column is at (pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1), pi(i)+1). This is one row above the bottom row of rectangle (since rectangle includes rows i..pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1)+1). The uncovered square's row pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1) is one less than the bottom row of rectangle, so it is not inside rectangle. However, we must also ensure that i <= pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1)+1? Actually i may be greater or less. Let's examine.\n\nGiven a permutation pi, we can pair each right-critical cell a_i with the below-critical cell b_{pi(i)+1} (the column immediately to the right of the uncovered square in row i). The rectangle formed will be a vertical line segment in column pi(i)+1 covering rows from min(i, pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1)+1) to max(i, pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1)+1). The uncovered square in that column is at row pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1). Since the rectangle includes rows i and pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1)+1, the uncovered square's row is either between them (if pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1) is between i and pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1)+1) or not. Let's check: pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1) is the row where the uncovered square is in column pi(i)+1. The rectangle includes rows i and pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1)+1. Since pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1)+1 = (row of uncovered square) + 1, the uncovered square's row is exactly one less than the bottom row of rectangle (if pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1)+1 > i). So the uncovered square is not inside rectangle. If i > pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1)+1, then rectangle includes rows pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1)+1 to i. The uncovered square's row pi^{-1}(pi(i)+1) is one less than the lower bound, so also not inside rectangle. So indeed the rectangle does not contain the uncovered square. So this pairing seems always safe: a right-critical cell and the below-critical cell in the same column (the column to the right of the uncovered square) can be paired into a vertical rectangle that does not contain any uncovered square. Similarly, we could pair each below-critical cell with the right-critical cell in the same row (the row below the uncovered square). But we have to ensure we don't double count.\n\nThus we can pair each right-critical cell a_i with b_{pi(i)+1} (except when pi(i)=n, there is no right-critical cell for that row). For the row where pi(i)=n, there is no right-critical cell, but there is a below-critical cell for column n? Actually column n may have a below-critical cell if pi^{-1}(n) != n. Since exactly one row has uncovered square in column n, that row is pi^{-1}(n). So below-critical cell for column n exists if pi^{-1}(n) != n, i.e., if the uncovered square in column n is not in row n. But there is exactly one row with uncovered square in column n; if that row is not n, then there is a below-critical cell for column n. Similarly, there is exactly one column with uncovered square in row n; that column is pi(n). If pi(n) != n, then there is a right-critical cell for column n? Actually right-critical cells correspond to rows where pi(i) != n. So row n may have pi(n) != n, then there is a right-critical cell for row n. So there will be n-1 right-critical cells and n-1 below-critical cells. The pairing a_i with b_{pi(i)+1} pairs each right-critical cell with a distinct below-critical cell? Let's check: For each i such that pi(i) != n, we pair a_i with b_{pi(i)+1}. Since pi is a bijection, the set of columns pi(i)+1 for i with pi(i) != n is exactly all columns except column n+1 (which doesn't exist) and column pi(i) = n? Actually if pi(i) = n, then pi(i)+1 = n+1, which is out of range, so we exclude that. So for each i with pi(i) != n, pi(i)+1 is a column in [2..n]. Since pi is a bijection, the mapping i -> pi(i)+1 is a bijection from rows i (excluding the one where pi(i)=n) to columns in [2..n] (excluding column n+1). But column 1 might not be covered? Let's examine: If pi(i) = 0? No, pi(i) in [1..n]. So pi(i)+1 ranges from 2 to n+1. Since pi(i) != n, pi(i)+1 ranges from 2 to n. So the set of columns used is exactly all columns except column 1? Actually column 1 corresponds to pi(i)=0, which cannot happen. So column 1 is not in the set. So the pairing maps each right-critical cell to a below-critical cell in column pi(i)+1, which ranges over columns 2..n, excluding column 1. So the below-critical cells in column 1 (i.e., b_1) might remain unpaired. Similarly, the right-critical cell for row n might be unpaired? Let's examine.\n\nWe have n-1 right-critical cells (rows except the one with pi(i)=n). For each such row i, we pair with below-critical cell b_{pi(i)+1}. Since pi(i)+1 ranges over columns 2..n, we pair with all below-critical cells except possibly b_1 (if column 1 is not of the form pi(i)+1). Indeed column 1 corresponds to pi(i)=0, which never occurs. So b_1 is not paired. Similarly, there is a below-critical cell for column 1 (b_1) unless pi^{-1}(1) = n (i.e., uncovered square in column 1 is in row n). If pi^{-1}(1) = n, then there is no below-critical cell for column 1. So b_1 may not exist. So the pairing may cover all critical cells except possibly one right-critical cell (if pi(n) != n) and one below-critical cell (if pi^{-1}(1) != n). Let's analyze.\n\nThe set of right-critical cells: rows i where pi(i) != n. That's all rows except the one where pi(i) = n. So there is exactly one row i0 such that pi(i0) = n, and that row has no right-critical cell. So there are n-1 right-critical cells.\n\nThe set of below-critical cells: columns j where pi^{-1}(j) != n. That's all columns except the one where pi^{-1}(j) = n, i.e., the column j0 where the uncovered square is in row n. So there is exactly one column j0 such that pi^{-1}(j0) = n, and that column has no below-critical cell. So there are n-1 below-critical cells.\n\nNow consider the pairing a_i with b_{pi(i)+1} for each i != i0 (i.e., for each right-critical cell). For each such i, pi(i) != n, so pi(i)+1 is a column in [2..n]. Is it possible that pi(i)+1 = j0 (the column with no below-critical cell)? If j0 = pi(i)+1 for some i, then b_{j0} 