the vertical rectangle \\\\(V_k\\\\).\\n\\nThus every square that is not one of the uncovered squares lies in exactly one of the rectangles \\\\(H_k\\\\) or \\\\(V_k\\\\).  The rectangles are pairwise disjoint, each avoids the uncovered squares, and there are \\\\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\\\) of them.\\n\\n#### 5.  Minimality\\nThe lower‑bound argument of Section 3 shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\\\(2n-2\\\\) tiles, while the explicit construction of Section 4 achieves exactly \\\\(2n-2\\\\) tiles.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n\\\\[\\\\boxed{2n-2}.\\\\]\\nFor the given grid \\\\(n=2025\\\\) we obtain\\n\\\\[2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048.\\\\]\\nHence Matilda must place at least and at most \\\\(4048\\\\) rectangular tiles, and this number is optimal.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Response to the Bug Report\\nThe report claims that for \\\\(n=3\\\\) a covering with only three tiles exists, which would contradict the lower bound \\\\(2n-2=4\\\\).  Exhaustive inspection of all six possible permutations of three rows shows that the complement of any permutation matrix consists of six unit squares that cannot be partitioned into three axis‑parallel rectangles without covering an uncovered square.  In each case at least four rectangles are required, confirming the lower bound.  The alleged three‑tile construction therefore does not exist, and the optimal number for \\\\(n=3\\\\) is indeed four, in agreement with the general formula \\\\(2n-2\\\\).\",\n  \"final_answer\": 4048\n}\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For