ocessed cell is a right‑critical cell \\\\(a_i\\\\), create a horizontal rectangle\\n     \\\\[H_i=\\\\{\\\\,(i,j)\\\\mid \\\\pi(i)<j<c_i\\\\},\\\\]\\n     where \\\\(c_i\\\\) is the smallest column greater than \\\\(\\\\pi(i)\\\\) whose square \\\\((i,c_i)\\\\) is already covered (if no such column exists, set \\\\(c_i=n+1\\\\)).  The rectangle therefore starts at the right‑critical cell \\\\(a_i\\\\) and extends rightwards as far as possible without intersecting any previously placed rectangle.\\n   * If the processed cell is a below‑critical cell \\\\(b_j\\\\), create a vertical rectangle\\n     \\\\[V_j=\\\\{\\\\,(i,j)\\\\mid r_j<i< n+1\\\\},\\\\]\\n     where \\\\(r_j\\\\) is the largest row smaller than \\\\(\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1\\\\) whose square \\\\((r_j,j)\\\\) is already covered (if no such row exists, set \\\\(r_j=0\\\\)).  The rectangle therefore starts at the below‑critical cell \\\\(b_j\\\\) and extends downwards as far as possible without intersecting any previously placed rectangle.\\n   Because we process cells in increasing \\\\(i+j\\\\), any rectangle that could intersect the one we are about to place has already been placed, so the stopping rule is well‑defined and guarantees that the new rectangle does **not** overlap any earlier rectangle.  Each new rectangle contains exactly one critical cell (its anchor) and cannot contain an uncovered square, because the anchor lies immediately to the right (or below) of the uncovered square in its row (or column) and the rectangle extends only in the opposite direction.\\n5. **Coverage proof.**  Let \\\\((i,j)\\\\) be any unit square that is not uncovered.\\n   * If \\\\(j>\\\\pi(i)\\\\) then \\\\((i,j)\\\\) lies to the right of the uncovered square in its own row.  The sum \\\\(i+j\\\\) is larger than the sum of the right‑critical cell \\\\(a_i\\\\) (which is \\\\(i+\\\\pi(i)+1\\\\)).  Consequently \\\\(a_i\\\\) is processed before \\\\((i,j)\\\\).  When \\\\(a_i\\\\) is processed, the horizontal rectangle \\\\(H_i\\\\) is extended rightwards until it meets a previously covered square; at that moment no square to the right of \\\\(a_i\\\\) is covered, so \\\\(H_i\\\\) reaches at least column \\\\(j\\\\) and therefore covers \\\\((i,j)\\\\).\\n   * If \\\\(j<\\\\pi(i)\\\\) then \\\\(i>\\\\pi^{-1}(j)\\\\); i.e. the square lies below the uncovered square in its column.  The sum \\\\(i+j\\\\) is larger than the sum of the below‑critical cell \\\\(b_j\\\\) (which is \\\\(\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1+j\\\\)).  Hence \\\\(b_j\\\\) is processed before \\\\((i,j)\\\\).  When \\\\(b_j\\\\) is processed, the vertical rectangle \\\\(V_j\\\\) is extended downwards until it meets a previously covered square; at that moment no square below \\\\(b_j\\\\) is covered, so \\\\(V_j\\\\) reaches at least row \\\\(i\\\\) and therefore covers \\\\((i,j)\\\\).\\n   In either case the square \\\\((i,j)\\\\) is covered by the rectangle created when the appropriate critical cell is processed.  Thus every non‑uncovered square belongs to exactly one of the constructed rectangles.\\n6. **Counting tiles.**  The algorithm creates one rectangle for each of the \\\\(2n-2\\\\) critical cells, so the total number of tiles is \\\\(2n-2\\\\).  By the lower‑bound argument this number is optimal.\\n7. **Conclusion.**  For \\\\(n=2025\\\\) the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n   \\\\[2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048.\\\\]\\n   Hence Matilda must place at least and at most \\\\(4048\\\\) tiles, and this number is achievable.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Detailed Solution ###\\n\\nLet \\\\(n=2025\\\\).  The grid consists of the set \\\\([n]\\\\times[n]\\\\) of unit squares, where \\\\([n]=\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots ,n\\\\}\\\\).\\n\\n#### 1.  Uncovered squares form a permutation matrix\\nBecause each row must contain exactly one uncovered square and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, there exists a bijection \\\\(\\\\pi:[n]\\\\to[n]\\\\) such that the uncovered squares are\\n\\\\[\\nU=\\\\{\\\\,(i,\\\\pi(i))\\\\mid i\\\\in[n] \\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nAll other squares are to be covered by tiles.\\n\\n#### 2.  Critical cells\\nFor every row \\\\(i\\\\) with \\\\(\\\\pi(i)\\\\neq n\\\\) define the *right‑critical* cell\\n\\\\[a_i=(i,\\\\pi(i)+1).\\\\]\\nFor every column \\\\(j\\\\) with \\\\(\\\\pi^{-1}(j)\\\\neq n\\\\) define the *below‑critical* cell\\n\\\\[b_j=(\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1,\\\\,j).\\\\]\\nThese cells are the unit squares that lie immediately to the right of an uncovered square (except in the last column) and immediately below an uncovered square (except in the last row).  Because \\\\(\\\\pi\\\\) is a permutation, exactly one row contains the uncovered square in column \\\\(n\\\\) and exactly one column contains the uncovered square in row \\\\(n\\\\).  Hence there are \\\\(n-1\\\\) right‑critical cells and \\\\(n-1\\\\) below‑critical cells, altogether\\n\\\\[|\\\\{a_i\\\\}|+|\\\\{b_j\\\\}|=2n-2.\\\\]\\nAll critical cells are distinct.\\n\\n#### 3.  Lower bound – a tile can contain at most one critical cell\\nConsider a legal rectangular tile \\\\(R\\\\).  Let its set of rows be an interval \\\\([r_1,r_2]\\\\) and its set of columns be an interval \\\\([c_1,c_2]\\\\) (the sides of a rectangle lie on grid lines, so the rows and columns it occupies are consecutive).\\n\\nSuppose that \\\\(R\\\\) contains two distinct critical cells.  Three possibilities arise.\\n\\n*Case 1 – two right‑critical cells.*  Let them be \\\\(a_i=(i,\\\\pi(i)+1)\\\\) and \\\\(a_k=(k,\\\\pi(k)+1)\\\\) with \\\\(i<k\\\\).  Then \\\\(i,k\\\\in[r_1,r_2]\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\pi(i)+1,\\\\pi(k)+1\\\\in[c_1,c_2]\\\\).  Because the column interval \\\\([c_1,c_2]\\\\) is contiguous, it contains every integer between \\\\(\\\\pi(i)+1\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\pi(k)+1\\\\).  If \\\\(\\\\pi(i)<\\\\pi(k)\\\\) then the column \\\\(\\\\pi(k)\\\\) lies between them, so the square \\\\((k,\\\\pi(k))\\\\) (which is uncovered) belongs to \\\\(R\\\\).  If \\\\(\\\\pi(i)>\\\\pi(k)\\\\) the same argument shows that \\\\((i,\\\\pi(i))\\\\) is in \\\\(R\\\\).  In either sub‑case \\\\(R\\\\) would contain an uncovered square, contradicting legality.\\n\\n*Case 2 – two below‑critical cells.*  This is symmetric to Case 1 after swapping the roles of rows and columns, and leads to the same contradiction.\\n\\n*Case 3 – one right‑critical cell \\\\(a_i=(i,\\\\pi(i)+1)\\\\) and one below‑critical cell \\\\(b_j=(\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1,\\\\,j)\\\\).*  The rectangle contains rows \\\\(i\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1\\\\) and columns \\\\(\\\\pi(i)+1\\\\) and \\\\(j\\\\).  If \\\\(j\\\\le \\\\pi(i)\\\\) then column \\\\(j\\\\) lies in the column interval, and together with row \\\\(i\\\\) the uncovered square \\\\((i,\\\\pi(i))\\\\) is inside \\\\(R\\\\).  If \\\\(j>\\\\pi(i)\\\\) then column \\\\(\\\\pi(i)\\\\) lies in the column interval, and together with row \\\\(\\\\pi^{-1}(j)\\\\) the uncovered square \\\\((\\\\pi^{-1}(j),j)\\\\) is inside \\\\(R\\\\).  Again a contradiction.\\n\\nThus **any legal tile can contain at most one critical cell**.\\nSince every critical cell must be covered, at least \\\\(2n-2\\\\) tiles are necessary.\\n\\n#### 4.  Greedy construction attaining the bound\\nWe now exhibit a tiling with exactly \\\\(2n-2\\\\) tiles.\\n\\nOrder the set of critical cells by the sum of their coordinates:\\n\\\\[\\\\text{order }\\\\; (i,j)\\\\;\\\\text{by increasing }\\\\;i+j.\\\\]\\nProcess the cells in this order and create a rectangle anchored at each cell.\\n\\n*If the processed cell is a right‑critical cell \\\\(a_i=(i,\\\\pi(i)+1)\\\\):*\\nDefine\\n\\\\[c_i=\\\\min\\\\{\\\\,j>\\\\pi(i)\\\\mid (i,j)\\\\text{ is already covered}\\\\},\\\\]\\nwith the convention that \\\\(c_i=n+1\\\\) if the set is empty.  Then set\\n\\\\[H_i=\\\\{\\\\,(i,j)\\\\mid \\\\pi(i)<j<c_i\\\\}.\\\\]\\nThe rectangle \\\\(H_i\\\\) is a horizontal strip in row \\\\(i\\\\); it contains the anchor \\\\(a_i\\\\) and extends rightwards until it would meet a square that has already been covered by a previously placed rectangle.\\n\\n*If the processed cell is a below‑critical cell \\\\(b_j=(\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1,\\\\,j)\\\\):*\\nDefine\\n\\\\[r_j=\\\\max\\\\{\\\\,i<\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1\\\\mid (i,j)\\\\text{ is already covered}\\\\},\\\\]\\nwith the convention that \\\\(r_j=0\\\\) if the set is empty.  Then set\\n\\\\[V_j=\\\\{\\\\,(i,j)\\\\mid r_j<i< n+1\\\\}.\\\\]\\nThe rectangle \\\\(V_j\\\\) is a vertical strip in column \\\\(j\\\\); it contains the anchor \\\\(b_j\\\\) and extends downwards until it would meet a previously covered square.\\n\\nBecause we process cells in increasing \\\\(i+j\\\\), any rectangle that could intersect the one we are about to place has already been placed.  Consequently the stopping points \\\\(c_i\\\\) and \\\\(r_j\\\\) are well defined and the new rectangle never overlaps an earlier one.  Each rectangle clearly contains exactly its anchor critical cell and no uncovered square: the anchor lies immediately to the right (or below) of the uncovered square in its row (or column), and the rectangle extends only in the opposite direction.\\n\\n#### 5.  Coverage proof\\nLet \\\\((i,j)\\\\) be a unit square that is not uncovered.\\n\\n*Case 1 – \\\\(j>\\\\pi(i)\\\\).*  Then \\\\((i,j)\\\\) lies to the right of the uncovered square in its own row.  The sum \\\\(i+j\\\\) is larger than the sum of the right‑critical cell \\\\(a_i\\\\) (which equals \\\\(i+\\\\pi(i)+1\\\\)).  Hence \\\\(a_i\\\\) is processed before \\\\((i,j)\\\\).  When \\\\(a_i\\\\) is processed, the horizontal rectangle \\\\(H_i\\\\) is extended rightwards until it meets a previously covered square.  At that moment no square to the right of \\\\(a_i\\\\) is covered (otherwise its covering rectangle would have a smaller sum and would therefore have been processed earlier, contradicting the definition of the order).  Consequently \\\\(H_i\\\\) reaches at least column \\\\(j\\\\) and therefore covers \\\\((i,j)\\\\).\\n\\n*Case 2 – \\\\(j<\\\\pi(i)\\\\).*  Then \\\\(i>\\\\pi^{-1}(j)\\\\); i.e. the square lies below the uncovered square in its column.  The sum \\\\(i+j\\\\) is larger than the sum of the below‑critical cell \\\\(b_j\\\\) (which equals \\\\(\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1+j\\\\)).  Thus \\\\(b_j\\\\) is processed before \\\\((i,j)\\\\).  When \\\\(b_j\\\\) is processed, the vertical rectangle \\\\(V_j\\\\) is extended downwards until it meets a previously covered square.  As before, at that moment no square below \\\\(b_j\\\\) is covered, so \\\\(V_j\\\\) reaches at least row \\\\(i\\\\) and covers \\\\((i,j)\\\\).\\n\\nIn either case the square \\\\((i,j)\\\\) is covered by the rectangle created when the appropriate critical cell is processed.  Therefore every non‑uncovered square belongs to exactly one of the constructed rectangles.\\n\\n#### 6.  Counting tiles\\nThe algorithm creates one rectangle for each of the \\\\(2n-2\\\\) critical cells, so the total number of tiles is \\\\(2n-2\\\\).  By the lower‑bound argument this number is optimal.\\n\\n#### 7.  Final answer for the given grid\\nFor the original problem \\\\(n=2025\\\\).  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n\\\\[2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048.\\\\]\\nMatilda must place at least and at most \\\\(4048\\\\) rectangular tiles, and this number can indeed be achieved.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Conclusion:** The minimum number of tiles required is \\\\(\\\\boxed{4048}\\\\).\",\n  \"final_answer\": 4048\n}"
