).\\n\\n*Case 2 \u2013 \\\\(j<\\\\pi(i)\\\\).*  Then \\\\(i>\\\\pi^{-1}(j)\\\\); i.e. the square lies below the uncovered square in its column.  The sum \\\\(i+j\\\\) is larger than the sum of the below\u2011critical cell \\\\(b_j\\\\) (which equals \\\\(\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1+j\\\\)).  Thus \\\\(b_j\\\\) is processed before \\\\((i,j)\\\\).  When \\\\(b_j\\\\) is processed, the vertical rectangle \\\\(V_j\\\\) is extended downwards until it meets a previously covered square.  As before, at that moment no square below \\\\(b_j\\\\) is covered, so \\\\(V_j\\\\) reaches at least row \\\\(i\\\\) and covers \\\\((i,j)\\\\).\\n\\nIn either case the square \\\\((i,j)\\\\) is covered by the rectangle created when the appropriate critical cell is processed.  Therefore every non\u2011uncovered square belongs to exactly one of the constructed rectangles.\\n\\n#### 6.  Counting tiles\\nThe algorithm creates one rectangle for each of the \\\\(2n-2\\\\) critical cells, so the total number of tiles is \\\\(2n-2\\\\).  By the lower\u2011bound argument this number is optimal.\\n\\n#### 7.  Final answer for the given grid\\nFor the original problem \\\\(n=2025\\\\).  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n\\\\[2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048.\\\\]\\nMatilda must place at least and at most \\\\(4048\\\\) rectangular tiles, and this number can indeed be achieved.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Conclusion:** The minimum number of tiles required is \\\\(\\\\boxed{4048}\\\\).\",\n  \"final_answer\": 4048\n}"
