ally the construction says rectangle V_j contains the anchor b_j = (π^{-1}(j)+1, j) = (2,1). So the rectangle includes (2,1) and extends downwards to row 3 (since row 1 is uncovered). But the rectangle includes (1,1) which is uncovered, which is not allowed. However the construction says the rectangle extends downwards until it meets a previously covered square, but also must not contain uncovered squares. The anchor is below-critical cell (2,1), which is immediately below the uncovered square (1,1). The rectangle V_1 includes rows from r_j+1 up to n (or until hitting a covered square). r_j = 0, so rows 1..3. That includes (1,1) which is uncovered. But perhaps the construction intends to include only rows > π^{-1}(j)+1? Actually definition: V_j = { (i,j) | r_j < i < n+1 }. That includes i from r_j+1 up to n. Since r_j = 0, includes i=1..n. That includes uncovered square (1,1). That would be illegal. However maybe they intend to start from π^{-1}(j)+1 (the anchor row) downwards, not from r_j+1. Let's re-express: The anchor is at row π^{-1}(j)+1, which is the below-critical cell. The rectangle should extend downwards from that anchor, not upward. So the set should be { (i,j) | π^{-1}(j)+1 ≤ i < c } maybe. The solution's definition seems ambiguous. Let's examine the text: \"Define r_j = max { i < π^{-1}(j)+1 | (i,j) is already covered } with convention r_j = 0 if empty. Then set V_j = { (i,j) | r_j < i < n+1 }.\" So they include all rows above the anchor as well, which would include the uncovered square if r_j < π^{-1}(j). Indeed r_j is the largest already covered row below the uncovered square? Actually π^{-1}(j) is the row of the uncovered square in column j. So π^{-1}(j)+1 is the row just below that uncovered square. So r_j is the largest row less than that anchor that is already covered. Since initially no squares covered, r_j = 0. Then V_j includes rows from 1 to n (including the uncovered square). That seems contradictory. However maybe they rely on the fact that