t of an uncovered square (except in the last column) and immediately below an uncovered square (except in the last row).  Because \\\\(\\\\pi\\\\) is a permutation, exactly one row contains the uncovered square in column \\\\(n\\\\) and exactly one column contains the uncovered square in row \\\\(n\\\\).  Hence there are \\\\(n-1\\\\) right\u2011critical cells and \\\\(n-1\\\\) below\u2011critical cells, altogether\\n\\\\[|\\\\{a_i\\\\}|+|\\\\{b_j\\\\}|=2n-2.\\\\]\\nAll critical cells are distinct.\\n\\n#### 3.  Lower bound \u2013 a tile can contain at most one critical cell\\nConsider a legal rectangular tile \\\\(R\\\\).  Let its set of rows be an interval \\\\([r_1,r_2]\\\\) and its set of columns be an interval \\\\([c_1,c_2]\\\\).\\n\\nSuppose that \\\\(R\\\\) contains two distinct critical cells.  Three possibilities arise.\\n\\n*Case 1 \u2013 two right\u2011critical cells.*  Let them be \\\\(a_i=(i,\\\\pi(i)+1)\\\\) and \\\\(a_k=(k,\\\\pi(k)+1)\\\\) with \\\\(i<k\\\\).  Then \\\\(i,k\\\\in[r_1,r_2]\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\pi(i)+1,\\\\pi(k)+1\\\\in[c_1,c_2]\\\\).  Because the column interval is contiguous, it contains every integer between \\\\(\\\\pi(i)+1\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\pi(k)+1\\\\).  If \\\\(\\\\pi(i)<\\\\pi(k)\\\\) then the column \\\\(\\\\pi(k)\\\\) lies between them, so the square \\\\((k,\\\\pi(k))\\\\) (which is uncovered) belongs to \\\\(R\\\\).  If \\\\(\\\\pi(i)>\\\\pi(k)\\\\) the same argument shows that \\\\((i,\\\\pi(i))\\\\) is in \\\\(R\\\\).  In either sub\u2011case \\\\(R\\\\) would contain an uncovered square, contradicting legality.\\n\\n*Case 2 \u2013 two below\u2011critical cells.*  This is symmetric to Case\u202f1 after swapping rows and columns, and leads to the same contradiction.\\n\\n*Case 3 \u2013 one right\u2011critical cell \\\\(a_i=(i,\\\\pi(i)+1)\\\\) and one below\u2011critical cell \\\\(b_j=(\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1,\\\\,j)\\\\).*  The rectangle contains rows \\\\(i\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1\\\\) and columns \\\\(\\\\pi(i)+1\\\\) and \\\\(j\\\\).  If \\\\(j\\\\le \\\\pi(i)\\\\) then column \\\\(j\\\\) lies in the column interval, and together with row \\\\(i\\\\) the uncovered square \\\\((i,\\\\pi(i))\\\\) is inside \\\\(R\\\\).  If \\\\(j>\\\\pi(i)\\\\) then column \\\\(\\\\pi(i)\\\\) lies in the column interval, and together with row \\\\(\\\\pi^{-1}(j)\\\\) the uncovered square \\\\((\\\\pi^{-1}(j),j)\\\\) is inside \\\\(R\\\\).  Again a contradiction.\\n\\nThus **any legal tile can contain at most one critical cell**.\\nSince every critical cell must be covered, at least \\\\(2n-2\\\\) tiles are necessary.\\n\\n#### 4.  Greedy construction attaining the bound\\nWe now exhibit a tiling with exactly \\\\(2n-2\\\\) tiles.\\n\\nOrder the set of critical cells by the sum of their coordinates:\\n\\\\[\\\\text{order }\\\\; (i,j)\\\\;\\\\text{by increasing }\\\\;i+j.\\\\]\\nProcess the cells in this order and create a rectangle anchored at each cell.\\n\\n*If the processed cell is a right\u2011critical cell \\\\(a_i=(i,\\\\pi(i)+1)\\\\):*\\nDefine\\n\\\\[c_i=\\\\min\\\\{\\\\,j>\\\\pi(i)\\\\mid (i,j)\\\\text{ is already covered}\\\\},\\\\]\\nwith the convention that \\\\(c_i=n+1\\\\) if the set is empty.  Then set\\n\\\\[H_i=\\\\{\\\\,(i,j)\\\\mid \\\\pi(i)<j<c_i\\\\}.\\\\]\\nThe rectangle \\\\(H_i\\\\) is a horizontal strip in row \\\\(i\\\\); it contains the anchor \\\\(a_i\\\\) and extends rightwards until it would meet a square that has already been covered by a previously placed rectangle.\\n\\n*If the processed cell is a below\u2011critical cell \\\\(b_j=(\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1,\\\\,j)\\\\):*\\nLet\\n\\\\[s_j=\\\\min\\\\{\\\\,i>\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1\\\\mid (i,j)\\\\text{ is already covered}\\\\},\\\\]\\nwith the convention that \\\\(s_j=n+1\\\\) if the set is empty, and let\\n\\\\[r_j=\\\\max\\\\{\\\\,i<\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1\\\\mid (i,j)\\\\text{ is already covered}\\\\},\\\\]\\nwith the convention that \\\\(r_j=0\\\\) if the set is empty.  Then set\\n\\\\[V_j=\\\\{\\\\,(i,j)\\\\mid \\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1\\\\le i<s_j\\\\}.\\\\]\\nThe rectangle \\\\(V_j\\\\) is a vertical strip in column \\\\(j\\\\); it contains the anchor \\\\(b_j\\\\) and extends downwards until it would meet a square that has already been covered.\\n\\nBecause we process cells in increasing \\\\(i+j\\\\), any rectangle that could intersect the one we are about to place has already been placed.  Consequently the stopping points \\\\(c_i\\\\) and \\\\(s_j\\\\) are well defined and the new rectangle never overlaps an earlier one.  Each rectangle clearly contains exactly its anchor critical cell and no uncovered square: the anchor lies immediately to the right (or below) of the uncovered square in its row (or column), and the rectangle extends only in the opposite direction.\\n\\n#### 5.  Coverage proof\\nLet \\\\((i,j)\\\\) be a unit square that is not uncovered.\\n\\n*Case 1 \u2013 \\\\(j>\\\\pi(i)\\\\).*  Then \\\\((i,j)\\\\) lies to the right of the uncovered square in its own row.  The sum \\\\(i+j\\\\) is larger than the sum of the right\u2011critical cell \\\\(a_i\\\\) (which equals \\\\(i+\\\\pi(i)+1\\\\)).  Hence \\\\(a_i\\\\) is processed before \\\\((i,j)\\\\).  When \\\\(a_i\\\\) is processed, the horizontal rectangle \\\\(H_i\\\\) is extended rightwards until it meets a previously covered square.  At that moment no square to the right of \\\\(a_i\\\\) is covered (otherwise its covering rectangle would have a smaller sum and would have been processed earlier, contradicting the definition of the order).  Consequently \\\\(H_i\\\\) reaches at least column \\\\(j\\\\) and therefore covers \\\\((i,j)\\\\).\\n\\n*Case 2 \u2013 \\\\(j<\\\\pi(i)\\\\).*  Then \\\\(i>\\\\pi^{-1}(j)\\\\); i.e. the square lies below the uncovered square in its column.  The sum \\\\(i+j\\\\) is larger than the sum of the below\u2011critical cell \\\\(b_j\\\\) (which equals \\\\(\\\\pi^{-1}(j)+1+j\\\\)).  Thus \\\\(b_j\\\\) is processed before \\\\((i,j)\\\\).  When \\\\(b_j\\\\) is processed, the vertical rectangle \\\\(V_j\\\\) is extended downwards until it meets a previously covered square.  As before, at that moment no square below \\\\(b_j\\\\) is covered, so \\\\(V_j\\\\) reaches at least row \\\\(i\\\\) and covers \\\\((i,j)\\\\).\\n\\nIn either case the square \\\\((i,j)\\\\) is covered by the rectangle created when the appropriate critical cell is processed.  Therefore every non\u2011uncovered square belongs to exactly one of the constructed rectangles.\\n\\n#### 6.  Counting tiles\\nThe algorithm creates one rectangle for each of the \\\\(2n-2\\\\) critical cells, so the total number of tiles is \\\\(2n-2\\\\).  By the lower\u2011bound argument this number is optimal.\\n\\n#### 7.  Final answer for the given grid\\nFor the original problem \\\\(n=2025\\\\).  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is\\n\\\\[2\\\\cdot2025-2=4048.\\\\]\\nMatilda must place at least and at most \\\\(4048\\\\) rectangular tiles, and this number can indeed be achieved.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Conclusion:** The minimum number of tiles required is \\\\(\\\\boxed{4048}\\\\).\",\n  \"final_answer\": 4048\n}"
