.\\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**\\n   For each row $i$ place at most two horizontal tiles:\\n   * If $\\\\pi(i)>1$, place a tile covering the squares $(i,1),(i,2),\\\\dots,(i,\\\\pi(i)-1)$.  This tile lies in $L$.\\n   * If $\\\\pi(i)<n$, place a tile covering the squares $(i,\\\\pi(i)+1),(i,\\\\pi(i)+2),\\\\dots,(i,n)$.  This tile lies in $R$.\\n   The tiles are disjoint because they occupy different rows, and together they cover every square that is not uncovered.  Exactly one row has $\\\\pi(i)=1$ (no left tile) and exactly one row has $\\\\pi(i)=n$ (no right tile), so the total number of tiles is $(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2$.\\n5. **Conclusion.** Both the lower bound and the explicit construction give the same number $2n-2$.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is $2n-2$.\\n\\nFor $n=2025$ we obtain\\n\\\\[2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\\\\]\\nThus the answer is \\\\boxed{4048}.\\n\\n---\\n\\n### Detailed Solution ###\\n\\nLet $n=2025$.  The $n\\\\times n$ grid consists of the set $[n]\\\\times[n]$, where $[n]=\\\\{1,2,\\\\dots ,n\\\\}$.\\n\\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix\\nBecause each row must contain exactly one uncovered unit square and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, there exists a bijection $\\\\pi:[n]\\\\to[n]$ such that the uncovered squares are\\n\\\\[\\nU=\\\\{\\\\,(i,\\\\pi(i))\\\\mid i\\\\in[n] \\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nAll other squares must be covered by tiles.\\n\\n#### 2.  Decomposition into two regions\\nFor a square $(i,j)$ define\\n\\\\[\\nL=\\\\{\\\\,(i,j)\\\\mid j<\\\\pi(i)\\\\},\\\\qquad R=\\\\{\\\\,(i,j)\\\\mid j>\\\\pi(i)\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nClearly $([n]\\\\times[n])\\\\setminus U = L\\\\cup R$ and $L\\\\cap R=\\\\varnothing$.\\n\\n**Claim.** Every legal tile is contained entirely in $L$ or entirely in $R$.\\n\\n*Proof.*  Suppose a tile $T$ contains a square $(i,j)$ with $j<\\\\pi(i)$ (so $(i,j)\\\\in L$) and also a square $(i',j')$ with $j'>\\\\pi(i')$ (so $(i',j')\\\\in R$).  Because $T$ is a rectangle, its set of rows is an interval $[r_1,r_2]$ and its set of columns is an interval $[c_1,c_2]$ with $c_1\\\\le j<\\\\pi(i)\\\\le c_2$ and $c_1\\\\le \\\\pi(i')<j'\\\\le c_2$.  Hence $\\\\pi(i)\\\\in[c_1,c_2]$ and $i\\\\in[r_1,r_2]$, so the uncovered square $(i,\\\\pi(i))\\\\in T$, contradicting legality.  \u220e\\n\\nThus the tiling problem separates into two independent subproblems: covering $L$ with tiles that stay inside $L$, and covering $R$ with tiles that stay inside $R$.\\n\\n#### 3.  A lower bound for $L$\\nFor each column $j\\\\in\\\\{1,\\\\dots ,n-1\\\\}$ define the *left\u2011critical* cell\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\ell_j\\\\;=\\\\;(\\\\pi^{-1}(j+1),\\\\,j).\\n\\\\]\\nBecause $\\\\pi$ is a permutation, the values $\\\\pi^{-1}(j+1)$ are all distinct; consequently the $\\\\ell_j$ are distinct and all belong to $L$ (indeed $j<j+1=\\\\pi(\\\\pi^{-1}(j+1))$).\\n\\n**Lemma.** A tile contained in $L$ can contain at most one left\u2011critical cell.\\n\\n*Proof.*  Assume a tile $T\\\\subseteq L$ contains two distinct left\u2011critical cells $\\\\ell_{j_1}=(i_1,j_1)$ and $\\\\ell_{j_2}=(i_2,j_2)$ with $j_1<j_2$.  Then the column interval of $T$ contains $j_2+1$.  Since $i_2=\\\\pi^{-1}(j_2+1)$, the square $(i_2,j_2+1)$ is uncovered (it belongs to $U$) and also lies in $T$, a contradiction.  \u220e\\n\\nThere are $n-1$ left\u2011critical cells, each of which must be covered, and each tile of $L$ can cover at most one of them.  Hence at least $n-1$ tiles are required to cover $L$.\\n\\n#### 4.  A lower bound for $R$\\nDually, for each row $i\\\\in\\\\{1,\\\\dots ,n-1\\\\}$ define the *right\u2011critical* cell\\n\\\\[\\n r_i\\\\;=\\\\;(i,\\\\,\\\\pi(i)+1).\\n\\\\]\\nThese $r_i$ are distinct (different rows) and belong to $R$ (since $\\\\pi(i)+1>\\\\pi(i)$).\\n\\n**Lemma.** A tile contained in $R$ can contain at most one right\u2011critical cell.\\n\\n*Proof.*  The argument is symmetric to the previous lemma: if a tile $T\\\\subseteq R$ contained $r_{i_1}=(i_1,j_1)$ and $r_{i_2}=(i_2,j_2)$ with $i_1<i_2$, then its row interval would contain $i_2$ and its column interval would contain $\\\\pi(i_2)$, forcing the uncovered square $(i_2,\\\\pi(i_2))\\\\in T$, impossible.  \u220e\\n\\nThus at least $n-1$ tiles are needed to cover $R$.\\n\\n#### 5.  Global lower bound\\nSince a tile cannot belong to both $L$ and $R$, the total number of tiles in any admissible tiling satisfies\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\#\\\\text{tiles}\\\\;\\\\ge\\\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n#### 6.  Construction achieving $2n-2$ tiles\\nFor each row $i$ we place at most two horizontal tiles:\\n\\n* If $\\\\pi(i)>1$, let\\n\\\\[\\nH_i^{\\\\text{left}}=\\\\{\\\\,(i,j)\\\\mid 1\\\\le j<\\\\pi(i)\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nThis tile lies entirely in $L$.\\n\\n* If $\\\\pi(i)<n$, let\\n\\\\[\\nH_i^{\\\\text{right}}=\\\\{\\\\,(i,j)\\\\mid \\\\pi(i)<j\\\\le n\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nThis tile lies entirely in $R$.\\n\\nThe tiles are pairwise disjoint because they occupy different rows.  Every square not in $U$ belongs either to $H_i^{\\\\text{left}}$ or to $H_i^{\\\\text{right}}$ for its row $i$, so the whole complement $([n]\\\\times[n])\\\\setminus U$ is covered.\\n\\nExactly one row has $\\\\pi(i)=1$ (no left tile) and exactly one row has $\\\\pi(i)=n$ (no right tile).  Hence the total number of tiles is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\#\\\\text{tiles}= (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\n\\\\]\\nThus the bound $2n-2$ is attainable.\\n\\n#### 7.  Conclusion\\nBoth the lower bound and the explicit construction give the same value $2n-2$.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is $2n-2$.\\n\\nFor $n=2025$ we obtain\\n\\\\[\\n2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\\n\\\\]\\nHence the answer is \\\\boxed{4048}.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Answer:** 4048\",\n  \"final_answer\": 4048\n}"
